{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-13ff1f06-7b50-4a48-b4e3-9b2373981186","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-806df7f8c60991c9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-xz7q2_e8/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E002.md","content":"# Investigate artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nDetermine how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The strategy flags this as an open question because the answer affects the HTTPRoute design and determines whether the main tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations — i.e., whether the routing can be handled entirely at the gateway level or requires server-side delegation support.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Evaluate gateway-level routing: an HTTPRoute that routes artifact API paths (e.g., the artifact-serving endpoints) from the main tracking server's host to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, with no change to the tracking server itself. Assess feasibility against the existing conditional Gateway API HTTPRoute pattern in the operator (runtime CRD discovery).\n- Evaluate tracking-server delegation: a configuration change on the main tracking deployment that delegates artifact operations to the dedicated instance, including what server-side support (if any) the tracking server would need and how the operator would propagate that configuration.\n- Compare the two mechanisms against the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern (CR spec → Helm values → rendered templates → Server-Side Apply) and document which artifacts each option requires (HTTPRoute templates, Deployment configuration, server settings).\n- Produce a recommended mechanism with a decision record covering the trade-offs (routing precision, operational surface, backwards compatibility for deployments that do not enable the split topology, and misconfiguration risk where artifact requests could bypass workspace validation).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A decision record recommends one routing mechanism (gateway-level path routing vs. tracking-server delegation) with documented trade-offs and evidence from the operator's existing routing and Helm rendering patterns.\n- The record states explicitly whether the main tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, so the server workstream knows whether delegation support is in its scope.\n- The record specifies the HTTPRoute design implications for the dedicated artifacts-only instance (host/path structure, relationship to the main tracking route) consistent with the operator's conditional Gateway API dependency.\n- The recommendation addresses the misconfiguration risk called out in the strategy (artifact requests bypassing workspace validation) and how the chosen mechanism prevents or detects it.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables the P1 HLRs \"MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\" and \"Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\" by fixing the routing design those deliverables implement.\n- Resolves the strategy's Open Question 2 (owner: MLflow Team).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":59295,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c411d79be451aa1","agent_id":"aca432ed42d3c5a72","session_id":"115c743c-228e-470f-acfa-50e91d621e74","uuid":"2f8ed4c1-76c1-4783-8a46-d3c45fbf1ecc","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:10:05.873Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
