{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-22c7b47e-4bd2-44a3-8248-aaf9b635e4e2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad19f6fa61f5348d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-xz7q2_e8/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E003.md","content":"# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nMake `--artifacts-only` composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests (tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized, non-artifact API endpoints stay disabled), and validates every artifact request against workspace membership before serving. No new server flag is introduced; the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Flag composition and initialization: allow `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` to be set together; when combined, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if missing) and initialize only the workspace provider needed for artifact validation. Each flag used individually must retain its current behavior and initialization sequence.\n- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: for each artifact request, resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g., `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`). Requests failing workspace validation receive HTTP 403. Kubernetes API mechanics of the authorization check are an implementation detail of the plugin, not prescribed here.\n- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than served without validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` (and any deployment not setting the workspace flags) retains current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys change meaning.\n- Audit logging: workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit entries in the server logs (P2 HLR; the telemetry channel may be redirected by PM/Security — see strategy Open Question 4).\n- Scope limitation per the strategy's risk mitigation: initial path resolution targets the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration) with its well-defined `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure; limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n- Performance measurement: measure and report the per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution (workspace store lookup plus auth plugin validation) so the caching decision (defer-or-launch, strategy Open Question 1) can be made from data; short-TTL caching of membership decisions is a follow-up if the measured overhead exceeds the agreed budget.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, the server initializes the workspace provider without initializing tracking or model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- Given a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server, the artifact is served — measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content (requires the auth plugin's workspace resolution functioning in the artifacts-only context).\n- Given a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running with the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied — measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's artifact request is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` set without `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error message.\n- Given each flag used individually (artifacts-only alone; workspaces-enabled tracking server alone), existing behavior is unchanged — measured by the existing test suites for both modes passing.\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs with enough context to audit (user/workspace/artifact path).\n- Artifact path resolution supports the S3 artifact store structure `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`; limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n- The per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and reported against the agreed latency budget, with a documented recommendation on whether caching is required at launch or deferred to a follow-up.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P0: \"`--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving\"\n- P0: \"Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\" (end-to-end behavior; the plugin-side resolution capability is delivered separately)\n- P0: \"Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied\"\n- P0: \"Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes\"\n- P2: \"Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs\" (bundled: the logging lives in the same denial code path as the P0 validation work)\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":60008,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c411d79be451aa1","agent_id":"aca432ed42d3c5a72","session_id":"115c743c-228e-470f-acfa-50e91d621e74","uuid":"47a8d5bd-28d7-4cac-bc31-cb4d8422d3d1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:10:23.019Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
