identically for both server types. Kubernetes API mechanics of the authorization check are implementation details of this package, not prescribed by the strategy.\n- Preserve the tracking server's existing (non-artifact) workspace resolution behavior — no regressions in workspace-scoped authorization for tracking, model registry, or prompt registry operations (all explicitly out of scope for change).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Workspace resolution resolves an experiment to its workspace assignment via the `kubernetes://` workspace store in a server context where tracking and model registry stores are not initialized — measured by a test exercising the plugin's resolution path in that context.\n- Artifact request paths following the `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure can be resolved to their parent experiment's workspace assignment without consulting a tracking store.\n- If refactoring was required: workspace resolution initializes through an independent path, and the tracking server's existing workspace resolution behavior is unchanged — measured by the plugin's and server's existing test suites passing.\n- The workspace-provider registration surface (entry point group, registry loading) is preserved, so both the tracking server and the artifacts-only server load the same provider without plugin-specific branching by the callers.\n- No change is required to the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar or `--app-name kubernetes-auth` configuration on either server deployment.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P1: \"Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\"\n- Supports (does not own) the P0 HLR \"Artifact requests … are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\" — the server-side validation delegates to this capability.\n- Addresses the strategy dependency \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin (internal, exists): may req