{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-847beb6141e6c82a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E005 (operator split-server topology) is oversized: the strategy''s\n10\t    own effort estimate allocates 2-3 sprints to the operator split-deployment work\n11\t    (dedicated Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing), which\n12\t    is multiple sprints and exceeds the ~2-week epic bound. Split it along its two\n13\t    P1 HLRs: (a) deploying the dedicated artifacts-only instance (Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute,\n14\t    workspace flag propagation, split-topology validation) and (b) routing artifact\n15\t    traffic from the main tracking deployment (the E002-gated routing mechanism work)\n16\t    - separable deliverables.'\n17\t- severity: major\n18\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n19\t  description: 'Artifact-path-to-workspace resolution is claimed by both E003 and\n20\t    E004: E003''s scope has the server resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment\n21\t    and look up the experiment''s workspace assignment via the workspace store, while\n22\t    E004''s scope and ACs assign experiment-to-workspace mapping (including from {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/\n23\t    paths) to the plugin, with near-identical artifact-path-resolution ACs in both\n24\t    epics. Assign path resolution and workspace lookup to a single owner (the plugin,\n25\t    per the strategy''s dependency note) and reduce the other epic to consuming the\n26\t    capability.'\n27\t- severity: minor\n28\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n29\t  description: E003's latency AC ('measured and reported against the agreed latency\n30\t    budget') depends on an externally agreed budget that does not exist yet (Open\n31\t    Question 1, owner PM/Engineering). Make the measurement unconditional and the\n32\t    budget comparison conditional, or record the budget dependency in Risks/Notes.\n33\t- severity: minor\n34\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n35\t  description: E003 open_questions=0 while E004 open_questions=-1 although both are\n36\t    gated by the same E001 investigation and E003's gate_failure_impact states its\n37\t    initialization path is adjusted by the outcome. The scope-flip (E004) vs approach-adjust\n38\t    (E003) distinction is arguable; align the values or strengthen the rationale.\n39\terror: null\n40\t---\n41\t## Review Summary\n42\t\n43\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n44\tRecommendation: revise\n45\t\n46\tContract validator: OK (no violations). Triage: none (full decomposition; 5 epics expected). No conditional branch files exist; none are required — both Investigations use bounded outcomes that change the scope/approach of main-plan epics (`rewrite` gates), which is a valid alternative to branch files.\n47\t\n48\tThe decomposition is strong on traceability, DAG discipline, gating metadata, and signal honesty. It fails on Epic Boundaries: one epic is oversized by the strategy's own effort estimate, and one capability (artifact-path-to-workspace resolution) is claimed by two sibling epics with near-identical acceptance criteria.\n49\t\n50\t## Criterion Details\n51\t\n52\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n53\tAll 8 HLRs are mapped in the traceability matrix: the four P0 HLRs to E003, the three P1 HLRs to E004/E005, and the P2 audit-logging HLR bundled into E003 with an explicit justification (same denial code path — incidental to the P0 validation work, not an independently deferrable feature; acceptable bundling). Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) gates E003 which carries the P0 HLRs; E002 (P1) gates only P1 work. The E004 priority inversion (P1 epic whose capability the end-to-end P0 403 behavior depends on) originates in the strategy's own HLR priorities, not in the DAG — E004 is not a DAG prerequisite of E003, and the decomposition surfaced it as health warning 1 rather than silently overriding strategy priorities. Correct handling.\n54\t\n55\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n56\tThe four edges (E001→E003, E001→E004, E002→E005) match the epic frontmatter `dependencies` exactly, and the `graph TD` diagram uses the dependency→dependent convention correctly. No cycles, no invalid references. Both Investigations are true gates (`gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` with action and fallback). The deliberate non-edges are correctly justified: E003∥E004 (separate codebases; the server builds against the plugin's existing, preserved interface) and E005∥E003 (the operator's flag references are configuration references per the Rule 13 exception — enforced by E005's \"using a server image that supports the combined flags\" AC gate, not a DAG edge — exactly the shape the rules mandate). No konflux-onboarding or license-validation epics exist, so the rule 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are vacuously satisfied. Critical path length 2 is shorter than the M-L heuristic (3-7), but the heuristics presume onboarding/docs chains; here both components are already in the platform, no docs-authoring epic is required (Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not Yes), and the three implementation streams genuinely parallelize after their gates. Serializing them to lengthen the path would be the defect, not the fix.\n57\t\n58\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n59\tComponent/team tuples are consistent (all MLflow / MLflow Team, matching the canonical component list's single MLflow entry), and the epic split follows the strategy's three codebases. Two major issues:\n60\t\n61\t1. **E005 is oversized.** The strategy's own effort estimate allocates \"Operator split-deployment topology (dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, artifact traffic routing): 2-3 sprints\" — and E005's scope is exactly that plus workspace-flag propagation, split-topology validation, and backwards compatibility. Two to three sprints is multiple sprints of work, well over the ~2-week epic bound. The epic is also splittable along its two P1 HLRs: (a) deploying the dedicated artifacts-only instance and (b) routing artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment (the E002-gated mechanism work) are separable deliverables.\n62\t2. **Duplicated capability ownership between E003 and E004.** E003's scope states the server will \"resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment ..., look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure\", while E004's scope and ACs assign experiment-to-workspace mapping — explicitly including resolution from `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` paths — to the plugin. Both epics carry a near-identical acceptance criterion on artifact-path resolution (E003: \"Artifact path resolution supports the S3 artifact store structure ...\"; E004: \"Artifact request paths following the `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure can be resolved to their parent experiment's workspace assignment ...\"). The path-resolution/workspace-lookup step is assigned to both the server and the plugin; this is a boundary drawn incorrectly, not incidental overlap. The strategy's dependency note assigns \"workspace resolution from artifact request paths\" to the plugin — pick one owner and reduce the other epic to consuming the capability.\n63\t\n64\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n65\tBoth Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream scope: E001's outcome flips E004 between verification-only and refactoring and adjusts E003's initialization path; E002's outcome determines E005's routing design and whether server-side delegation support is needed. All three gated epics have `gated_by` set, `gate_failure_impact` populated with both `action` and `fallback_approach`, and every `gated_by` target is in the epic's direct `dependencies`. No epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`. Implementations produce concrete artifacts. No misclassifications.\n66\t\n67\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n68\tType-appropriate signal sets are used everywhere (investigation_signals on E001/E002, ai_signals on E003-E005) and rationale files exist for all five epics with substantive justifications. Investigation signals match the rubric: E001's local_runnability=1 (pip-installable Python, exercisable with a stubbed Kubernetes client) and cluster_hardware_dependence=0 (gating question answerable from source) are correct; E002's local_runnability=0 / cluster_hardware_dependence=-1 correctly reflect that only the peripheral routing-behavior validation needs a live Gateway API cluster while the core determination is desk-resolvable. Implementation signals are honestly scored, including unfavorable ones (E004's repo_access=0 and architecture_claims=0 given the missing plugin architecture-context file; human_process_gates=-1 across the board matching the pending strategy gates). One arguable value: E003's open_questions=0 vs E004's -1 for the same E001 gate — defensible on the scope-flip (E004) vs approach-adjust (E003) distinction, which the rationales do articulate, but noted as a minor for the revise pass.\n69\t\n70\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n71\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's proposed acceptance criteria (the six strategy ACs map onto E003/E005 ACs almost verbatim, with measurable HTTP 200/403/503 outcomes). No rule-mandated ACs apply (no replacement/rollback epic, no docs-authoring epic, no konflux chain), and E003/E005 carry backwards-compatibility ACs preserving current behavior, which is the right analogue for this change. No draft-ID references to sibling epics appear in any epic body. One minor: E003's final AC (\"measured and reported against the agreed latency budget\") depends on an externally agreed budget that does not exist yet (Open Question 1, owner PM/Engineering) — the measurement should be unconditional and the budget comparison conditional, or the dependency recorded in Risks/Notes.\n72\t\n73\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n74\tFull scope coverage: flag composition, workspace validation with 403, fail-closed 503, single-tenant/backwards compatibility, operator split topology, artifact traffic routing, plugin resolution, and P2 audit logging all map to epic scope and ACs. All three SME corrections are honored (no new flag or CR field anywhere in the epic set; split-deployment topology rather than a config toggle; Kubernetes API mechanics explicitly left as plugin implementation detail in both E003 and E004). Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all four open questions are dispositioned (two become the Investigations, latency and audit channel become ACs plus health warnings), all four risks map to mitigations in epic scope (S3 path scoping, E001 investigation, E005 operator validation, latency measurement), and the assumptions are each handled. Integration testing is distributed as ACs across E003/E004/E005 with an explicit health warning rather than silently dropped. The docs-authoring rule is not triggered (Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not Yes), and the absence of onboarding-chain epics is consistent with both codebases having Konflux pipelines per architecture context.\n75\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-860f5d5e1e4d853d","agent_id":"afbd8680b7250ff64","session_id":"115c743c-228e-470f-acfa-50e91d621e74","uuid":"464a3f8f-e08f-4391-b0e0-b7d1c545b9b3","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:17:44.315Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-847beb6141e6c82a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
