{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b6c23d7fdd81e89","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins is coupled\n15\t    to tracking store initialization, the server's workspace-provider-only initialization\n16\t    path for artifacts-only mode is adjusted to the refactored plugin initialization\n17\t    (or a server-side workaround) before workspace validation can be wired; the flag-composition\n18\t    and backwards-compatibility work is unaffected.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: -1\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tMake `--artifacts-only` composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests (tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized, non-artifact API endpoints stay disabled), and validates every artifact request against workspace membership before serving. No new server flag is introduced; the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Flag composition and initialization: allow `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` to be set together; when combined, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if missing) and initialize only the workspace provider needed for artifact validation. Each flag used individually must retain its current behavior and initialization sequence.\n45\t- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: for each artifact request, resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g., `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`). Requests failing workspace validation receive HTTP 403. Kubernetes API mechanics of the authorization check are an implementation detail of the plugin, not prescribed here.\n46\t- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than served without validation.\n47\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` (and any deployment not setting the workspace flags) retains current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys change meaning.\n48\t- Audit logging: workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit entries in the server logs (P2 HLR; the telemetry channel may be redirected by PM/Security — see strategy Open Question 4).\n49\t- Scope limitation per the strategy's risk mitigation: initial path resolution targets the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration) with its well-defined `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure; limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n50\t- Performance measurement: measure and report the per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution (workspace store lookup plus auth plugin validation) so the caching decision (defer-or-launch, strategy Open Question 1) can be made from data; short-TTL caching of membership decisions is a follow-up if the measured overhead exceeds the agreed budget.\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, the server initializes the workspace provider without initializing tracking or model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n55\t- Given a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server, the artifact is served — measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content (requires the auth plugin's workspace resolution functioning in the artifacts-only context).\n56\t- Given a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n57\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running with the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied — measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n58\t- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's artifact request is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n59\t- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` set without `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error message.\n60\t- Given each flag used individually (artifacts-only alone; workspaces-enabled tracking server alone), existing behavior is unchanged — measured by the existing test suites for both modes passing.\n61\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs with enough context to audit (user/workspace/artifact path).\n62\t- Artifact path resolution supports the S3 artifact store structure `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`; limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n63\t- The per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and reported against the agreed latency budget, with a documented recommendation on whether caching is required at launch or deferred to a follow-up.\n64\t\n65\t## HLR Traceability\n66\t\n67\t- P0: \"`--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving\"\n68\t- P0: \"Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\" (end-to-end behavior; the plugin-side resolution capability is delivered separately)\n69\t- P0: \"Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied\"\n70\t- P0: \"Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes\"\n71\t- P2: \"Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs\" (bundled: the logging lives in the same denial code path as the P0 validation work)\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-860f5d5e1e4d853d","agent_id":"afbd8680b7250ff64","session_id":"115c743c-228e-470f-acfa-50e91d621e74","uuid":"beb8d7c3-ec0b-408b-af0c-f4b797dedd1d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:17:55.897Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b6c23d7fdd81e89","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
