opology reconcile identically to before — measured by the operator's existing test suite passing.\n- The deployed split topology passes the end-to-end workspace-validated artifact serving scenarios (member served HTTP 200, non-member denied HTTP 403, workspace store unreachable denied fail-closed) against the operator-deployed instances, using a server image that supports the combined flags.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P1: \"MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\"\n- P1: \"Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\"\n- Supports the strategy's split-server topology direction from the Staff Engineer / SME Input (split deployment, not a configuration toggle).","new_string":"# MLflow operator deployment of the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, forming the deployment half of a split-server topology: the main tracking deployment handles experiment tracking, model registry, and prompt registry, while the dedicated artifacts-only instance handles artifact serving with workspace validation. The operator propagates workspace configuration to the dedicated instance using the same mechanisms it already uses for the main tracking deployment. Configuring the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to this dedicated instance is a separable deliverable tracked as its own epic; this epic delivers the dedicated instance itself.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New Helm chart templates (rendered through the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern: MLflow CR spec → Helm values → embedded chart templates → Server-Side Apply) for the artifacts-only Deployment, its Service, and its HTTPRoute, separate from the main tracking Deployment's resources.\n- Workspace configuration propagation to the artifacts