{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d5ff3918a125c8c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009\n11\t\n12\tWorkspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage\n15\t\n16\tNot below threshold (effort M-L, 4-7 sprints; three affected components across three distinct codebases; multiple architecturally distinct work streams). Not documentation-only (all affected components require code changes). Full decomposition follows.\n17\t\n18\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n19\t\n20\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. All three corrections are already incorporated into the strategy's Technical Approach; the decomposition follows them:\n21\t\n22\t| SME direction | How it is honored |\n23\t|---|---|\n24\t| No new flag — combine `--artifacts-only` with existing `--enable-workspaces` | Epics for the MLflow server describe flag composition only; no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag or `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field appears anywhere in the decomposition |\n25\t| Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle | The operator epics cover a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment (split into two epics along the strategy's two operator HLRs after review — the strategy's effort estimate allocates 2-3 sprints to this work, exceeding the ~2-week epic bound) |\n26\t| SSAR detail is over-specified | Epics state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics; kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` unchanged |\n27\t\n28\t## Epic List\n29\t\n30\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n31\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate plugin workspace-resolution initialization dependencies | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow operator deployment of the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n38\t\n39\t## Component Mapping Notes\n40\t\n41\tThe canonical RHAI component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains a single `MLflow` entry covering the MLflow server, the MLflow operator, and the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins package. All epic `component` frontmatter values are therefore `MLflow`. Epic boundaries below follow the strategy's Affected Components table (three distinct codebases: `red-hat-data-services/mlflow`, `red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator`, and the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` package), which is the boundary signal Rule 1 uses; the canonical-name collapse is a labeling constraint only.\n42\t\n43\tComponent existence per architecture context: `mlflow.md` and `mlflow-operator.md` exist (both in platform — no onboarding chains per Rule 20). `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` has no architecture-context file, but the strategy's dependency table lists it as internal and existing (v1.5.0, consumed by the MLflow image), and it is owned by the MLflow Team — it is treated as an existing component to modify, not a new component to provision. Flagged as a health warning (repo mapping unverified).\n44\t\n45\t## Dependency DAG\n46\t\n47\t```mermaid\n48\tgraph TD\n49\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: plugin init dependencies (P0)\"] --> E003[\"E003 Server: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving (P0)\"]\n50\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Plugin: workspace resolution in artifacts-only context (P1)\"]\n51\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: artifact traffic routing mechanism (P1)\"] --> E005[\"E005 Operator: dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment (P1)\"]\n52\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 Operator: artifact traffic routing from main tracking deployment (P1)\"]\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\t## DAG Justification\n56\t\n57\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n58\t|------|------|-----------|\n59\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether the workspace store / plugin workspace resolution can initialize without tracking stores determines how the server initializes only the workspace provider in artifacts-only mode. True gate, action `rewrite`: if resolution is coupled to full server initialization, the server's initialization approach changes (or the plugin is refactored first). Bounded outcomes (2): (A) not coupled — server wiring proceeds as described; (B) coupled — plugin refactor (E004) becomes required and the server init path is adjusted. Outcomes change scope/approach of existing epics, not the epic set, so a `rewrite` gate on main-plan epics is used instead of branch files. |\n60\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The same determination fixes the plugin epic's scope: verification only (not coupled) vs. refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path (coupled). True gate, action `rewrite`. |\n61\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The routing mechanism determines the dedicated instance's HTTPRoute design (host/path structure, relationship to the main tracking route), one of this epic's three template deliverables. True gate, action `rewrite`. Bounded outcomes (2): (A) gateway-level routing — the dedicated instance's HTTPRoute path-shares the main tracking host; (B) tracking-server delegation — the dedicated instance gets its own host/route. Scope/approach changes, not epic existence → `rewrite` gate, no branch files. |\n62\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The routing mechanism (gateway-level path routing vs. tracking-server delegation) determines whether this epic is an operator-managed HTTPRoute configuration or delegation configuration on the main tracking Deployment (with server-side support coordinated with the server workstream). True gate, action `rewrite`. Same two bounded outcomes; scope/approach changes, not epic existence → `rewrite` gate, no branch files. |\n63\t| E003 ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rules 13/14/15 | Separate codebases. The server consumes the plugin's existing workspace-provider interface (entry point group / WorkspaceStoreRegistry) — including artifact-path-to-workspace resolution, solely owned by the plugin epic per the strategy's dependency note — which E004 preserves in both outcomes; E004 does not produce a new build-against artifact. Integration is enforced by acceptance criteria on E003 (end-to-end 403/200 behavior requires the plugin capability), not a DAG edge. |\n64\t| E005 ∥ E006 (no edge) | Rule 13 exception, Rule 15 | Same repo, different areas (new artifacts-only chart templates vs. routing configuration on the main tracking side) → parallel per Rule 15. The routing configuration references the dedicated instance's Service — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact — so the coupling is enforced by AC gates on E006 (routed-path scenarios validated against an operator-deployed dedicated instance), never a DAG edge. |\n65\t| E005/E006 ∥ E003/E004 (no edge) | Rule 13 exception, Rule 14 | The operator's Helm templates, flags, and routing configuration are configuration references to the server's capability (flags on a container command), not a build-against artifact — the same class as an image digest. Enforced by AC gates on E005/E006 (deployed topology passes workspace-validated serving scenarios using a server image that supports the combined flags), never by a DAG edge (mirrors Rule 10's reasoning). Different repo, no shared artifacts. |\n66\t\n67\t### Investigation decisions (Step 3 scan of open questions, assumptions, risks)\n68\t\n69\t| Unknown (source) | Decision | Handling |\n70\t|---|---|---|\n71\t| Does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability? (Open Question 3; Assumption 2; Risk \"Auth plugin coupling\") | Answer changes what the plugin epic and the server initialization do | **E001 Investigation**, gating E003/E004 |\n72\t| How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing, and does the tracking server need a configuration change to delegate artifact operations? (Open Question 2) | Answer changes the operator epics' approach (dedicated-instance HTTPRoute design and the routing mechanism) and potentially adds server-side scope | **E002 Investigation**, gating E005/E006 |\n73\t| Acceptable latency overhead per artifact request / is caching required at launch? (Open Question 1) | Implementation of workspace validation proceeds identically; caching is an additive optimization already noted as a risk mitigation and deferrable to a follow-up | Acceptance criterion on E003 (measure and report per-request overhead); health warning |\n74\t| Audit telemetry channel for workspace denials — OTLP, Kubernetes audit log, or server log? (Open Question 4) | The P2 HLR already specifies \"through server logs\"; the channel choice changes logging-statement scope, not which epics exist | Acceptance criterion on E003 scoped to server logs per the HLR; health warning that PM/Security may redirect the channel |\n75\t| Flags composable without breaking either flag's individual init sequence (Assumption 1) | Resolved by doing the work; failure = fix-and-retry | Acceptance criteria on E003 (each flag individually still works) |\n76\t| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes (Assumption 4) | The operator epic's work is exactly adding those templates; failure = adjust templates | Risk note on E005, not an Investigation |\n77\t| Artifact request paths carry enough information to resolve workspace membership (Assumption 5; Risk \"Artifact path resolution complexity\") | Strategy already scopes the mitigation: S3 artifact store with `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure | Acceptance criterion on E003 (S3 path structure supported; non-standard roots documented as a limitation) |\n78\t| Single-tenant deployments unaffected (Assumption 3) | Backwards-compatibility requirement, not an unknown | Acceptance criterion on E003 |\n79\t\n80\tRule 19 (safety-critical → fail-mode + security Investigations) was evaluated and not triggered: the strategy enforces tenant isolation but the fail-mode behavior is fully specified up front (fail-closed, HTTP 503 when the workspace store is unreachable, HTTP 403 on denials) and the workspace/RBAC model itself is explicitly out of scope and unchanged. There is no unresolved fail-mode or security-design question for an Investigation to resolve; generating one would reclassify already-answered questions (Rule 4).\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n83\t\n84\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n85\t|-----|----------|---------|\n86\t| `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E003 |\n87\t| Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 (end-to-end behavior; enabled by E004's plugin capability) |\n88\t| Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E003 |\n89\t| Single-tenant / no-workspace deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E003 |\n90\t| Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 |\n91\t| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E006 |\n92\t| Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | P1 | E004 |\n93\t| Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving | P2 | E003 (bundled: logging statements live in the same denial code path as the P0 validation work — same implementation surface, not independently deferrable) |\n94\t\n95\tAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphans. No redundant/overlapping HLR pairs were found (each HLR names a distinct surface: server flag composition/validation, operator topology, plugin resolution, audit logging).\n96\t\n97\t## Priority Inheritance\n98\t\n99\t- E001 gates E003, which carries four P0 HLRs → E001 is P0.\n100\t- E002 gates E005 and E006 (P1 HLRs only) → E002 is P1.\n101\t- E003 is P0 (four P0 HLRs; the P2 audit HLR is bundled, not split — same code path).\n102\t- E004 is P1 per its HLR. Note: E003 and E004 are parallel (no DAG edge), so E004 does not formally block P0 work, but the end-to-end P0 denial behavior depends on E004's capability — flagged as a health warning below.\n103\t- E005 and E006 are P1 per their HLRs.\n104\t\n105\t## Health Warnings (non-blocking)\n106\t\n107\t1. **Priority inversion risk (strategy-level)**: the plugin workspace-resolution HLR is P1, but the P0 HLR \"requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\" only behaves correctly end-to-end once the plugin resolution works in the artifacts-only context. The decomposition keeps the strategy's priorities (E004 = P1) but recommends tracking E004 at P0 criticality during planning.\n108\t2. **Audit channel ambiguity**: the P2 HLR specifies server logs, but Open Question 4 (owned by PM/Security) may redirect audit events to OTLP or the Kubernetes audit log. E003's audit AC is scoped to server logs per the HLR; a channel change would add scope to E003 only.\n109\t3. **Latency target undefined**: no latency budget is specified for per-request workspace validation (Open Question 1). Caching is treated as a measurable follow-up decision (AC on E003: measure and report overhead), not as launch-blocking scope.\n110\t4. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins repo mapping unverified**: no architecture-context file exists for the plugin; it is treated as an existing internal component per the strategy's dependency table (\"internal, exists\", v1.5.0). If the team's source repo for the plugin is not accessible, E004's repo-access assumption fails.\n111\t5. **Integration testing folded into epic ACs**: the strategy's effort estimate calls out 1-2 sprints of integration testing (multi-tenant scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed edge cases, split-server routing). This work is distributed as acceptance criteria across E003/E004/E005/E006 rather than a separate epic (same team; the boundary axes in Rule 2 do not include a testing axis).\n112\t6. **Pending human process gates**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level; no onboarding-chain epics were generated because both codebases already have Konflux pipelines per architecture context (recent-changes records show konflux-central syncs).\n113\t\n114\t## Tiered Delivery\n115\t\n116\tNot applicable. All dependencies (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy) are internal and existing per the strategy's dependency table; there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The work naturally parallelizes after the two Investigations: server work (E003), plugin work (E004), and operator work (E005, E006) can proceed concurrently on their respective gates.\n117\t\n118\t## Revision Notes (post-review)\n119\t\n120\tCorrections applied from the adversarial review (`artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomp-review.md`):\n121\t\n122\t1. **E005 split (Epic Boundaries, major)**: the operator epic was oversized — the strategy's effort estimate allocates 2-3 sprints to the split-deployment work. Split along the strategy's two P1 HLRs: E005 now deploys the dedicated artifacts-only instance (Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute, workspace flag propagation, split-topology validation), and the new E006 delivers artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment (the E002-gated routing mechanism work). Both remain gated by E002; E005 ∥ E006 per Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) with the Service reference handled as an AC gate (Rule 13 exception).\n123\t2. **Artifact-path-to-workspace resolution single ownership (Epic Boundaries, major)**: the capability was claimed by both E003 (server) and E004 (plugin). Per the strategy's dependency note, E004 is now the sole owner of artifact-path-to-workspace resolution (including the S3 `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` scoping and its documented limitations, moved from E003); E003 is reduced to consuming the capability through the plugin's workspace-provider interface.\n124\t3. **E003 latency AC (Acceptance Criteria, minor)**: the measurement is now unconditional; the budget comparison is conditional on a latency budget being agreed (strategy Open Question 1, owner PM/Engineering).\n125\t4. **E003 open_questions signal rationale (AI Implementability Scoring, minor)**: the 0-vs-(-1) distinction against E004 is now explicitly justified in E003's signal rationale (approach-adjust within a settled design vs. scope-flip between verification and refactoring); signal values unchanged.\n126\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-952f1b81690916df","agent_id":"a6112ae6c5a7eb8d3","session_id":"115c743c-228e-470f-acfa-50e91d621e74","uuid":"bcb45058-e338-4b97-80be-2434af02b4af","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:25:22.751Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d5ff3918a125c8c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-p