{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6c5cfb55548012f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins is coupled\n15\t    to tracking store initialization, the server's workspace-provider-only initialization\n16\t    path for artifacts-only mode is adjusted to the refactored plugin initialization\n17\t    (or a server-side workaround) before workspace validation can be wired; the flag-composition\n18\t    and backwards-compatibility work is unaffected.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: -1\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tMake `--artifacts-only` composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests (tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized, non-artifact API endpoints stay disabled), and validates every artifact request against workspace membership before serving, delegating artifact-path-to-workspace resolution and authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. No new server flag is introduced; the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Flag composition and initialization: allow `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` to be set together; when combined, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if missing) and initialize only the workspace provider needed for artifact validation. Each flag used individually must retain its current behavior and initialization sequence.\n45\t- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: for each artifact request, delegate workspace resolution and authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`), which resolves the artifact request path to its parent experiment and looks up that experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store. Artifact-path-to-workspace resolution is owned by the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workstream (per the strategy's dependency note); this epic consumes that capability through the plugin's workspace-provider interface and does not implement path resolution itself. Requests failing workspace validation receive HTTP 403. Kubernetes API mechanics of the authorization check are an implementation detail of the plugin, not prescribed here.\n46\t- Fail-closed behavior: when workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than served without validation.\n47\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` (and any deployment not setting the workspace flags) retains current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys change meaning.\n48\t- Audit logging: workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit entries in the server logs (P2 HLR; the telemetry channel may be redirected by PM/Security — see strategy Open Question 4).\n49\t- Performance measurement: measure and report the per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution (workspace store lookup plus auth plugin validation) so the caching decision (defer-or-launch, strategy Open Question 1) can be made from data; short-TTL caching of membership decisions is a follow-up if a latency budget has been agreed and the measured overhead exceeds it.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, the server initializes the workspace provider without initializing tracking or model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n54\t- Given a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server, the artifact is served — measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content (requires the auth plugin's workspace resolution functioning in the artifacts-only context).\n55\t- Given a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n56\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only server running with the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied — measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n57\t- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's artifact request is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n58\t- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` set without `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error message.\n59\t- Given each flag used individually (artifacts-only alone; workspaces-enabled tracking server alone), existing behavior is unchanged — measured by the existing test suites for both modes passing.\n60\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs with enough context to audit (user/workspace/artifact path).\n61\t- The per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and reported unconditionally. If a latency budget has been agreed (strategy Open Question 1, owner PM/Engineering), the report compares the measured overhead against that budget; either way it includes a documented recommendation on whether caching is required at launch or deferred to a follow-up.\n62\t\n63\t## HLR Traceability\n64\t\n65\t- P0: \"`--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving\"\n66\t- P0: \"Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\" (end-to-end behavior; artifact-path-to-workspace resolution is owned by the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workstream and consumed here through the plugin's interface)\n67\t- P0: \"Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied\"\n68\t- P0: \"Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes\"\n69\t- P2: \"Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs\" (bundled: the logging lives in the same denial code path as the P0 validation work)\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-952f1b81690916df","agent_id":"a6112ae6c5a7eb8d3","session_id":"115c743c-228e-470f-acfa-50e91d621e74","uuid":"d2a8f32b-1c68-4127-9736-19765e761674","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:25:30.627Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6c5cfb55548012f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
