{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e1668413347d08f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E006\n3\ttitle: Artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated\n4\t  artifacts-only instance\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n6\tcomponent: MLflow\n7\tteam: MLflow Team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P1\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the routing investigation selects gateway-level path routing,\n16\t    the work is an operator-managed HTTPRoute configuration; if it selects tracking-server\n17\t    delegation, configuration support for delegating artifact operations is added\n18\t    to the main tracking deployment in coordination with the server workstream. Either\n19\t    bounded outcome is implemented within this epic; the dedicated artifacts-only\n20\t    Deployment, Service, and workspace flag propagation are unaffected.\n21\tai_signals:\n22\t  change_specificity: 0\n23\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n24\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n25\t  existing_foundation: 1\n26\t  open_questions: -1\n27\t  external_dependency: 0\n28\t  human_process_gates: -1\n29\t  repo_access: 1\n30\t  architecture_claims: 1\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: High\n34\tai_implementability_score: 3\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\tbranch: null\n37\t---\n38\t# Artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n39\t\n40\t## Description\n41\t\n42\tConfigure the main tracking deployment in the MLflow operator's split-server topology so that artifact traffic is routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance. The routing mechanism — gateway-level path routing via HTTPRoute, or tracking-server delegation propagated as configuration on the main tracking deployment — is fixed by the outcome of the routing-mechanism investigation, and this epic implements whichever mechanism it selects.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\t- Implement artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the mechanism selected by the routing investigation: either an HTTPRoute that routes artifact API paths from the main tracking server's host to the dedicated instance, or tracking-server delegation configuration propagated by the operator to the main tracking Deployment.\n47\t- If tracking-server delegation is selected, coordinate the required server-side configuration support with the MLflow server workstream (the investigation's decision record states whether such support is needed).\n48\t- Keep routing configuration scoped to CRs that enable the split topology: deployments that do not opt in retain their current routing behavior, with no artifact traffic diverted.\n49\t- Preserve workspace validation on the routed path: the routing configuration must not allow artifact requests to reach artifact serving without workspace validation (the misconfiguration risk called out in the strategy); follow the prevention/detection approach documented in the investigation's decision record.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- The main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the selected routing mechanism — measured by artifact requests against the main tracking entry point being served by the dedicated instance.\n54\t- The routing implementation matches the routing investigation's decision record (selected mechanism, HTTPRoute design or delegation configuration) — measured by review against the record.\n55\t- Artifact requests routed through the main tracking entry point receive the same workspace-validated outcomes as requests made directly against the dedicated instance — measured by HTTP 200 for workspace members and HTTP 403 for non-members on routed artifact requests, validated against an operator-deployed dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n56\t- Deployments whose MLflow CRs do not enable the split topology retain current routing behavior — measured by the operator's existing test suite passing and no routing resources created for CRs that have not opted in.\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- P1: \"Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\"\n61\t- Implements the outcome of the strategy's Open Question 2 (owner: MLflow Team) once the routing mechanism is selected.\n62\t- Consumes the dedicated artifacts-only instance delivered by the operator deployment workstream as a configuration reference (the routing targets that instance's Service) — enforced by the routed-path acceptance criteria above, not a build dependency.\n63\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-952f1b81690916df","agent_id":"a6112ae6c5a7eb8d3","session_id":"115c743c-228e-470f-acfa-50e91d621e74","uuid":"150f47d0-eefc-4ab3-87c5-62c3ae6b6d71","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:25:35.442Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e1668413347d08f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
