itional latency measurement (with the budget comparison correctly conditional on a budget existing). E005 mirrors strategy ACs 5-6 (distinct Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute presence; exact container command flags) and adds misconfiguration rejection and conditional CRD discovery checks. No AC states an external schedule constraint (no lab turnaround or review-board cadence entries). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — cross-epic couplings are expressed as workstream/decision-record references with AC gates. No replacement, konflux-chain, or docs-authoring epics exist, so no rule-mandated ACs of those kinds apply; backwards compatibility is covered by the flag-gating ACs on E003, E005, and E006.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: every capability, all six proposed acceptance criteria, all four risks (with their mitigations placed — S3 path scoping and documented limitations in E004, plugin-coupling validation in E001, operator misconfiguration rejection in E005, latency measurement in E003), all five assumptions (each with an explicit disposition in the Investigation decisions table), and all four open questions (OQ1 latency AC, OQ2 E002, OQ3 E001, OQ4 audit AC scoped to server logs with a health warning on channel redirection). Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so no docs-authoring epic is required (Rule 11). Integration testing is distributed as ACs across E003-E006 rather than a separate epic — acceptable for a single team and disclosed as a health warning. One minor observation: if E002 selects tracking-server delegation, the server-side delegation support in the MLflow server has no explicitly assigned owning epic (E006 coordinates \"with the server workstream\"; E003's scope covers only the artifacts-only server). The conditional scope is acknowledged — E002's AC requires the decision record to state whether server-side support is needed, and E006's gate fallback names the coordination